# Seeds of the adjudication corpus on which the decision-graph adjudicator
# intentionally differs from the iterative adjudicator that recorded
# adjudication_corpus.golden. Each line gives the seed, the category and the
# digests of the current resolution (turn 1, turn 2); a seed may combine
# categories with "+". The categories are:
#
# cut_supporters       Report only. A supporter dislodged during the turn is
#                      listed in the combat's cut supporters; the previous
#                      adjudicator listed it only when the turn resolved in a
#                      single iteration.
# paradox              No resolution satisfies the rules: the cycle's joins and
#                      dispersions are cancelled and its attacks resolved
#                      without them. The previous adjudicator kept a
#                      dislodgement or a failure from an earlier iteration.
# legacy_inconsistent  The previous result violated the rules: an attack
#                      turned back before a head-to-head loser stopped
#                      contesting its destination, or a dislodgement kept from
#                      an earlier iteration. The current result is the
#                      consistent one.
# two_resolutions      Two resolutions satisfy the rules; the orders succeed in
#                      the adjudicator's fixed order whenever one allows it.
# allied_attack_dispersion
#                      Rule fix: a dispersion may arrive on a territory won by
#                      an attack of its own player, as a join already could;
#                      the previous adjudicator treated it as contested,
#                      against the GDD's definition of a contested destination.
# dispersion_execution Execution fix: allied troops stacked onto a dispersing
#                      army stay on its origin instead of vanishing, a
#                      dispersed troop fuses with the army that remains on its
#                      destination, a chain handed to another army keeps its
#                      orders consistent, and joins and dispersions read
#                      attacked territories as the adjudicator does. Seeds on
#                      which the previous adjudicator errored are pinned here.
# legacy_error         The previous adjudicator failed on this turn (combat that
#                      did not converge); the result is pinned from here on.
#
# The other categories are cases where the previous result depended on the
# order of its iterations.
